  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Colorado recorded $9.8B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state received 16,642 awards with an average award size of $589K, and obligations fell 39.1% year over year. The Department of Defense accounted for the largest share of buying activity with $5.8B in obligations. Aircraft Manufacturing led industries with $1.5B, followed by Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) with $1.2B and Engineering Services with $833M. SIERRA NEVADA COMPANY, LLC received the most obligations among vendors with $1.9B, followed by LOCKHEED MARTIN CORP with $1.6B and ALLIANCE FOR ENERGY INNOVATION, LLC with $648M. Annual obligations peaked at $16.1B in FY25 and are reported at $9.8B for FY26 to date. These figures are contract obligations and reflect fiscal year to date totals, not full-year results.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Why are FY2026 to date obligations lower than FY2025?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 39.1% year over year. FY25 totaled $16.1B and FY26 is $9.8B to date. The Department of Defense obligated $5.8B in FY2026 to date, maintaining a dominant share of current-year spending."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors dominate federal spending in Colorado?",
        "body": "Aircraft Manufacturing is the largest industry with $1.5B in obligations. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) recorded $1.2B, and Engineering Services recorded $833M. SIERRA NEVADA COMPANY, LLC leads vendors with $1.9B, followed by LOCKHEED MARTIN CORP with $1.6B and ALLIANCE FOR ENERGY INNOVATION, LLC with $648M."
      }
    ]
  },
